# Repository conventions for {{ project_name }}

This file expands on the boundaries in `AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md`. The rule of thumb: anything that fits in 5 bullets goes in `AGENTS.md`; anything longer goes here.

## Layout

```
src/                  # production code
tests/                # mirrors src/ structure
docs/                 # design docs, ADRs
scripts/              # one-off and operational scripts
.claude/              # agent harness — see .claude/README if present
```

{% if language == 'python' -%}
## Python conventions

- Type hints on all public functions. `from __future__ import annotations` at the top of every module.
- Pydantic models for any structured data crossing a boundary (HTTP, file, DB).
- `@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)` for value objects.
- Logging via `structlog` if added; otherwise stdlib `logging` with `__name__` logger per module.
- No bare `except:`. Catch the specific exception. Re-raise unless you can actually handle it.
- Tests use pytest. Fixtures in `conftest.py`. Parametrize over `(input, expected, id)` triples.
- Naming: `snake_case` for functions/vars, `PascalCase` for classes, `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for constants, `_leading_underscore` for module-private.
{% endif %}

{% if language == 'typescript' -%}
## TypeScript conventions

- Strict mode on. No `any`. If you'd reach for `any`, write a type or use `unknown` and narrow.
- ESM imports, no CommonJS in new code.
- Functional components for React; hooks named `use*`.
- Tests use {% if framework == 'next' %}Vitest{% else %}Jest or Vitest, matching what's already there{% endif %}.
- Async functions over Promise chains. Don't mix.
- Naming: `camelCase` for variables/functions, `PascalCase` for types/components, `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for constants.
{% endif %}

{% if language == 'go' -%}
## Go conventions

- Standard project layout: `cmd/<binary>/`, `internal/`, `pkg/` (only if truly reusable across projects).
- Errors wrapped with `fmt.Errorf("...: %w", err)`. Never just return the inner error without context.
- Interfaces defined where they're consumed, not where they're implemented.
- No `panic` in library code. `panic` is only for genuinely unrecoverable invariant violations.
- Tests as `<file>_test.go`. Table-driven tests preferred.
{% endif %}

{% if language == 'rust' -%}
## Rust conventions

- `#![warn(clippy::pedantic)]` in `lib.rs` / `main.rs`. Suppress specific lints with justification, never blanket.
- `Result<T, E>` over panicking. Custom error types with `thiserror`.
- `async-trait` only when you need dyn-dispatched async. Otherwise keep it static.
- Tests in `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` blocks for unit; `tests/` directory for integration.
{% endif %}

## Workflow

- One task per Claude session. For parallel work, create a git worktree (`git worktree add ../{{ project_name }}-<feature> -b feat/<feature>`) and start a fresh Claude session there.
- `/plan` before non-trivial work. The plan goes through review before any code is written.
- `/review` before `/commit`. `/techdebt` at end of session.
- Commits: Conventional Commits, one logical change per commit, no AI attribution.
- PRs: small (~100-300 line diff), one feature, squash-merge.

## Things not to do

- Don't introduce a new dependency without asking. Open a discussion first.
- Don't refactor unrelated code "while you're here." Separate commit, separate PR.
- Don't add tests that pass when you delete the implementation. (Per Simon Willison: tests must fail when you revert the change they were written for.)
- Don't use `--dangerously-skip-permissions` unless you're inside a sandbox.

## When to update this file

Update `repo-conventions/SKILL.md` when:
- You correct Claude on the same convention twice in one week.
- A new pattern stabilizes in the codebase that Claude wouldn't infer.
- A failure mode keeps recurring.

Don't update it for one-off cases. Don't write a 500-line style guide here. The shorter this stays, the more it gets read.
